TI  - Economic evaluation of tirzepatide, semaglutide, and liraglutide in the treatment of obese and overweight patients without diabetes: a short-term cost-effectiveness analysis in Iran.
AU  - Reiszadeh Negin
AU  - Radmanesh Ramin
AU  - Heshmat Ramin
AU  - Mohammadi Hamed
AU  - Darvishi Ali
JO  - Journal of diabetes and metabolic disorders
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s40200-026-02114-z
AB  - GLP-1 and dual GLP-1/GIP receptor agonists have improved obesity treatment, but their high cost requires economic evaluation to guide resource allocation. To assess the short-term cost-effectiveness of subcutaneous tirzepatide, semaglutide, and liraglutide for treating adults with overweight or obesity without diabetes in Iran. A decision-tree model was developed to compare tirzepatide, semaglutide, and liraglutide over 68 weeks in adults aged ≥ 18 years with BMI ≥ 30 kg/m² or ≥ 27 kg/m² with at least one additional weight-related complication, excluding those with diabetes, from the payer perspective. Clinical effectiveness, adverse event (AE), and treatment discontinuation data were obtained from published literatures. Quality-adjusted life years (QALYs) were estimated based on BMI changes and AE-related disutility. Direct medical costs included drug acquisition and AE management costs. Deterministic and probabilistic sensitivity analyses were performed. Compared with semaglutide, tirzepatide generated greater health benefits but at higher costs, resulting in an incremental cost-effectiveness ratio of US$34,423.89/QALY. Liraglutide was dominated by semaglutide. Semaglutide was cost-effective at the Iranian willingness-to-pay threshold of US$ (Purchasing power parity (PPP) 15,558.5/QALY, equal to one time GDP per capita in 2026. Sensitivity analyses confirmed the robustness of the findings. Semaglutide was the cost-effective injectable GLP-1-based therapy in Iran. Although tirzepatide provided greater health benefits, its higher cost limited its cost-effectiveness at current prices. The online version contains supplementary material available at https://doi.org/10.1007/s40200-026-02114-z.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42894545/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42850698
TI  - Weight-Loss Outcomes After Sleeve Gastrectomy Versus Tirzepatide in a Minority Cohort With Severe Obesity: Results From a Propensity Score Matched Cohort.
AU  - Manueli Laos Emiliano G
AU  - Fontaine-Nicola Andres
AU  - Serafica Bianca
AU  - Zhang Lily
AU  - Layden Brian T
AU  - Berger Reed
AU  - Orda Carlos Agustin
AU  - Schlottmann Francisco
AU  - Pirzada Amber
AU  - Masrur Mario A
JO  - Clinical obesity
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/cob.70116
AB  - While GLP-1 and GIP receptor agonists like semaglutide and tirzepatide have revolutionized pharmacologic obesity treatment, metabolic and bariatric surgery (MBS) continues to demonstrate superior weight loss and cost-effectiveness. Current evidence shows tirzepatide outperforms semaglutide with over 20% weight reduction, yet most clinical trials lack ethnic diversity, limiting the generalizability of these outcomes. This is a retrospective, single-centre study that evaluates 12-month weight loss results comparing tirzepatide and SG within a diverse, urban patient population. 12-month weight loss outcomes were compared between patients receiving tirzepatide and those undergoing sleeve gastrectomy (SG). Primary endpoints included changes in BMI, excess weight loss and total weight loss (TWL) percentage at 3, 6 and 12 months, following ASMBS reporting standards to evaluate treatment efficacy and comparative outcomes. Linear mixed models revealed statistically significant differences in weight loss between SG and tirzepatide cohorts across all time points (p < 0.001). By 12 months, the SG group was associated with greater weight loss than tirzepatide in this selected matched cohort with a 30% TWL and a 12 kg/m2 BMI reduction, compared to 12% TWL and 7 kg/m2 for tirzepatide. SG was associated with greater weight loss than tirzepatide in this selected matched cohort, especially for eligible patients in ethnic minority groups. However, tirzepatide serves as a vital non-invasive alternative, requiring individualized treatment decisions based on patient preference, cost and specific metabolic goals.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42850698/
